addObject: must refuse nil, the way its own two neighbours already do.

-[__NSCFArray insertObject:atIndex:] and -[__NSCFArray replaceObjectAtIndex:withObject:] both raise
Attempt to insert nil object, which is what macOS does. addObject: alone passed the NULL straight
to CFArrayAppendValue.

A NULL in a CFArray is not a stored nil, it is undefined behaviour with a delayed fuse: the array
callbacks are handed NULL, every later read gives an object back to a caller that cannot tell,
and the crash lands somewhere with no relation to the mistake. iA Writer added a nil bookmark here
and died four layers later inside removeObjectForKey: on a dictionary it had never touched. Had
this raised, the exception would have named the site.

This is a behaviour change with a roster-wide blast radius, so it is deliberately NOT folded into
the bookmark fix that made it visible.

--- a/NSArray.m
+++ b/NSArray.m
@@ -2097,6 +2097,18 @@
 
 - (void)addObject:(id)object
 {
+    /* Both neighbours here already refuse nil as macOS does. Storing the NULL instead moves the
+     * crash to an unrelated frame: iA Writer added a nil bookmark through this method and died
+     * four layers later on a nil dictionary key. */
+    if (object == nil)
+    {
+        CFStringRef reason = CFStringCreateWithFormat(kCFAllocatorDefault, NULL,
+                                                      CFSTR("Attempt to insert nil object at index %ld"),
+                                                      (long) CFArrayGetCount((CFArrayRef)self));
+        @throw [NSException exceptionWithName:NSInvalidArgumentException
+                                       reason:(NSString *)reason
+                                     userInfo:nil];
+    }
     if (_CFArrayIsMutable((CFArrayRef)self))
     {
         CFArrayAppendValue((CFMutableArrayRef)self, (const void *)object);
